---
import Scene from '../Scene.astro';
import Tex from '../Tex.astro';
import Slider from '../Slider.astro';
import Readout from '../Readout.astro';
import Segmented from '../Segmented.astro';
import Action from '../Action.astro';
---
<Scene id="vectors" figure="square" label="Two arrows u and v leave the origin in 3D. In Dot mode, v casts a shadow onto u and an arc marks the angle between them. In Cross mode, u and v span a parallelogram and a third arrow, u cross v, stands perpendicular to it with length equal to its area. In Plane mode, the arrows run from P(1,1,1) to Q(2,0,1) and R(2,1,0), and their cross product is the normal of the plane x + y + z = 3.">
  <div class="beat" data-beat="Two arrows">
    <p>A vector is an arrow: a length and a direction, nothing else. In space it has three components, <Tex t="\cA{u} = \langle u_1, u_2, u_3 \rangle"/>, one for each axis.</p>
    <p>There are two useful ways to multiply two vectors. One gives a number, the other gives a new vector. Both are about the angle between <Tex t="\cA{u}"/> and <Tex t="\cE{v}"/>.</p>
    <p class="aside">Drag the figure to turn it. The sliders set the components of <Tex t="\cE{v}"/>.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="beat" data-beat="Dot: a shadow">
    <p>Shine a light straight down onto the line of <Tex t="\cA{u}"/>. The <span class="k-c2">shadow</span> of <Tex t="\cE{v}"/> has signed length <Tex t="|\cE{v}|\cos\theta"/>. The dot product is that shadow times the length of <Tex t="\cA{u}"/>:</p>
    <Tex display t="\cA{u}\cdot\cE{v} = u_1v_1 + u_2v_2 + u_3v_3 = |\cA{u}|\,|\cE{v}|\cos\theta"/>
    <p>The left side is pure arithmetic and the right side is pure geometry, and they always agree. Watch the readout as <Tex t="\cE{v}"/> moves. When <Tex t="\cE{v}"/> stands at a right angle to <Tex t="\cA{u}"/>, the shadow vanishes and the dot product is <Tex t="0"/>.</p>
    <p class="aside">A classic check: for <Tex t="P(2,-6,-4)"/>, <Tex t="Q(4,0,-8)"/>, <Tex t="R(12,-4,-10)"/>, <Tex t="\vec{PQ}\cdot\vec{QR} = 16 - 24 + 8 = 0"/>, so the triangle has a right angle at <Tex t="Q"/>.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="beat" data-beat="Cross: an area">
    <p>The cross product <Tex t="\cB{u\times v}"/> is a vector. Its direction is perpendicular to both <Tex t="\cA{u}"/> and <Tex t="\cE{v}"/>, and its length is the area of the <span class="k-c3">parallelogram</span> they span:</p>
    <Tex display t="|\cB{u\times v}| = |\cA{u}|\,|\cE{v}|\sin\theta = \cC{\text{area}}"/>
    <p>Parallel vectors span no area, so their cross product is the zero vector. Perpendicular vectors span the most.</p>
    <p class="aside">For example, <Tex t="\langle 4,5,-3\rangle\times\langle 2,1,-3\rangle = \langle -12, 6, -6\rangle"/>, and dotting it with either factor gives <Tex t="0"/>. Its length is <Tex t="\sqrt{216} = 6\sqrt6"/>, so the triangle with those two vectors as sides has area <Tex t="3\sqrt6"/>, half the parallelogram.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="beat" data-beat="Right-hand rule">
    <p>Two directions are perpendicular to a parallelogram, up and down. Which one? Curl the fingers of your right hand from <Tex t="\cA{u}"/> toward <Tex t="\cE{v}"/>, and your thumb points along <Tex t="\cB{u\times v}"/>.</p>
    <p>Now <Tex t="\cE{v}"/> swings around in the floor plane. While it turns counterclockwise from <Tex t="\cA{u}"/>, the arrow points up. The moment <Tex t="\cE{v}"/> passes <Tex t="\cA{u}"/>, the area shrinks to zero and the arrow flips down. So order matters: <Tex t="\cE{v}\times\cA{u} = -\,\cA{u}\times\cE{v}"/>.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="beat" data-beat="A plane">
    <p>A plane is pinned down by one point <Tex t="P"/> and one direction <Tex t="\cB{n}"/> that sticks straight out of it. A point <Tex t="X"/> lies on the plane exactly when the arrow from <Tex t="P"/> to <Tex t="X"/> is perpendicular to <Tex t="\cB{n}"/>, a dot product equal to zero.</p>
    <Tex display t="\cB{n}\cdot(X - P) = 0 \iff a(x-x_0) + b(y-y_0) + c(z-z_0) = 0"/>
    <p>Given three points, the cross product hands you the normal. The example here takes <Tex t="P(1,1,1)"/>, <Tex t="Q(2,0,1)"/>, <Tex t="R(2,1,0)"/>: <Tex t="\vec{PQ}\times\vec{PR} = \langle 1,1,1\rangle"/>, so the plane is <Tex t="x + y + z = 3"/>. Move <Tex t="R"/> with the sliders and the plane follows.</p>
  </div>
  <Tex slot="takeaway" display t="\cA{u}\cdot\cE{v} = |\cA{u}||\cE{v}|\cos\theta \qquad |\cB{u\times v}| = |\cA{u}||\cE{v}|\sin\theta"/>
  <div slot="overlay" class="scene-overlay vec-overlay"><span data-vec-eq aria-live="off"></span></div>
  <Fragment slot="controls">
    <Segmented name="mode" label="Product" options={[['dot', 'Dot'], ['cross', 'Cross'], ['plane', 'Plane']]}/>
    <Action name="swing" label="Swing v" icon="play"/>
    <Readout name="r1" label="u · v" color="c2"/>
    <Readout name="r2" label="θ" color="c3"/>
    <Slider name="v1" label="v₁" min={-2} max={2} step={0.05} value={0.4} color="c5"/>
    <Slider name="v2" label="v₂" min={-2} max={2} step={0.05} value={1.8} color="c5"/>
    <Slider name="v3" label="v₃" min={-2} max={2} step={0.05} value={1} color="c5"/>
  </Fragment>
</Scene>
<style is:global>
  #vectors .vec-overlay{top:auto;bottom:14px;right:18px}
  #vectors [data-vec-eq]{font-family:'Courier New',monospace;font-size:13px;color:var(--ink);background:color-mix(in srgb,var(--calc-figure) 82%,transparent);padding:4px 8px;margin-left:-8px;align-self:flex-start}
  #vectors [data-vec-eq] b{font-weight:600}
</style>
